"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for any crisis at any official desk — consulates, airlines, insurance lines, hospitals abroad. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> Panic speaks in stories; desks run on facts. Every phrase here converts fear into information — what happened, what's left, what's the deadline, what can I do. Calm isn't a feeling you wait for; it's a sentence structure you decide to use.</div>

        <h3>🧊 Turning panic into information</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the opener — problem plus request for the process. Complete it:<br><br>"I've lost my passport — what do I do ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I've lost my passport — what do I do first?"</b><br>Notice what this ISN'T: the story of the market, the crowd, the sick feeling. It's the problem in five words and then the best question in any crisis — "what do I do first?" — which hands you the expert's map instead of your panic's. There is always a first step, and the person behind the glass always knows it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I've lost ___ — what do I do first?" Passports, wallets, phones, data. Name it, then ask for step one.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the full damage report, in one line. Complete it:<br><br>"Everything went with the bag — cards, passport, the ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Everything went with the bag — cards, passport, the lot."</b><br><b>The lot</b> = everything, all of it — the compact British way to close a list. The officer's first need is the size of the problem, so give the inventory upfront: what's gone, and (next breath) what you've already done about it. "I've frozen the cards and filed a police report" turned you, in one sentence, from a victim into a case that's already moving.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "___ went — ___, ___, the lot. Here's what I've done so far." The damage report shape, for burglaries, crashes, and IT disasters alike.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> putting your deadline on the table immediately. Complete it:<br><br>"I fly home on Friday — is that still ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I fly home on Friday — is that still possible?"</b><br>The deadline is the single fact that shapes the entire plan — it's the difference between a normal application and an emergency document — and desks can't plan around a deadline they haven't heard. Say it early, as a question, and you get the honest odds ("tight, but not impossible") plus the route that fits inside it. Hide it, or mention it on Thursday, and no procedure on earth can save your seat.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "My deadline is ___ — is that still possible?" Repairs, applications, medical results. Deadline first; plan second.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> asking for what CAN be done, not mourning what can't. Which one?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "So there's no way to get a real passport by Friday? None at all?"</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "What can you issue me in that time?"</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "This is a disaster. What am I supposed to do?"</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "What can you issue me in that time?"</b><br>A argues with reality — the weeks-long passport is not coming, and re-asking won't shrink it. C hands your problem to the officer as an emotion. B is the crisis question that always works: given the constraint, what IS available? The answer here was the emergency travel document — a thing you didn't know existed until you asked what existed. In every bureaucracy there's a faster, smaller version of what you need. This question finds it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "What can you do in that time?" Flights, visas, repairs, print deadlines — ask for the possible, not the perfect.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🧰 Offering everything you've still got</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> volunteering your evidence before it's demanded. Complete it:<br><br>"I've got a photo of it on my phone — does that ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I've got a photo of it on my phone — does that help?"</b><br>The traveller's cheapest insurance is a photographed passport page in the cloud — and the phrase that cashes it in is this one. "Does that help?" is the right ending: it lets the expert tell you what your scraps are worth ("not proof on its own, but it makes the checks quick instead of slow"). Offer everything — photos, references, screenshots. You are not qualified to know which of your scraps is gold; they are.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I've got ___ — does that help?" And tonight, genuinely: photograph your passport, ID and insurance card, and put them in the cloud.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> mobilising home. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>sent over</span><span>I can get documents</span><span>from home.</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I can get documents sent over from home."</b><br>You are not one person at a window — you're a network, and the desk should know it. Someone at home with your spare keys can scan a birth certificate tonight; someone can pay a fee online against a case number. Naming your resources ("my sister has keys to my flat") lets the officer build them into the plan on the spot. Stranded is mostly a feeling; the network is real.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I can get ___ sent over from home." Documents, money, confirmations — say who, and how fast.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the money problem, stated without shame. Complete it:<br><br>"What will it cost — and how do I pay with ___ ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"What will it cost — and how do I pay with no cards?"</b><br>The embarrassing question that must be asked, because the answer always exists — desks that serve robbed travellers have met robbed wallets before. Ask both halves together: the amount AND the mechanism. Out came two routes you'd never have guessed: online payment against a case number, and a cash transfer office two streets over. The people who don't ask sit outside the answer all afternoon.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "What will it cost, and how can I pay, given ___?" Name the constraint; let them route around it.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🕐 Using the wait, and covering the risk</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> converting helpless waiting into a job list. Complete it:<br><br>"While the checks run — is there anything I can be ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"While the checks run — is there anything I can be doing?"</b><br>The question that separates the passengers from the crew. There's almost always a parallel track — photos, the airline call, the charged phone — that shaves a day off the critical path, and officials hand these jobs gladly to anyone who asks. Bonus effect, not small: doing something is the actual cure for the panic. Hostel-ceiling-staring is not.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Is there anything I can be doing while we wait?" Visa queues, hospital corridors, job applications — ask for the parallel track.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the what-if, arranged while everything's still fine. Complete it:<br><br>"If something changes over the ___, who do I ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"If something changes over the weekend, who do I call?"</b><br>Thursday "usually" holds — and "usually" is exactly why you ask this on Tuesday, while the person who knows the answer is right in front of you. Out came the card with two numbers, including the out-of-hours line that's answered on Saturdays. The moment to arrange the emergency channel is before the emergency; afterwards, you're googling helplines from a hostel at midnight.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "If ___ goes wrong, who do I call — and does that number answer at weekends?" The fallback question, asked in the sunshine.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the human close — naming what they did for you. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="This morning you were panicking at a market. Now there's a plan. Close the window well…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "I was starting to panic this morning, honestly — thank you."</b><br>The small honesty ("I was panicking") is what makes the thanks real — you're telling him what the last twenty minutes actually changed. Consular officers spend careers being shouted at by the frightened; the ones who say this get remembered, and remembered kindly matters when your file is one of forty on Thursday. Note his gift back: "Thursday will probably hold." That's not in any procedure.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I was starting to panic, honestly — thank you." For everyone who ever turned your worst morning into a plan.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten are your crisis kit, anywhere on earth. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"I've got a photo of it — does that help?" — do you actually have cloud photos of your passport, ID and insurance? If not: tonight.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="What you have, what you'll photograph tonight…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"I can get ___ sent over from home." — who is your person? Who has keys, answers fast, and could scan a document at midnight?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your network, named…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"What do I do first?" — think of your last small crisis. What was step one — and how long did panic delay it?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The crisis, the first step, the delay…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="everyday_echoes_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every Everyday Echoes lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the window, out loud — what do I do first → cards, passport, the lot, and here's the police reference → I fly Friday, still possible? → what can you issue in that time → photo on my phone, does that help → documents from home → how do I pay with no cards → anything I can be doing → who do I call at the weekend → I was starting to panic, thank you. Ten sentences between stranded and Thursday afternoon.</div>""",

